<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- POGIL-style activity, Day 12 (~50 minutes). Follows the antiderivatives / natural-log -->
<!-- activity (Activity 11), which ends by DEFINING ln(x) = int_1^x (1/t) dt and asking for ln(1) -->
<!-- and the domain. Per the "we didn't finish" convention, this activity does not assume those -->
<!-- last two questions were reached: it restates the definition and recovers both facts in a -->
<!-- brief remark, so nothing is lost if Activity 11 stopped early. -->

<!-- FORMAT: the first half is INTERACTIVE LECTURE, not group discovery. The definition, remarks -->
<!-- and theorem below are on the students' page as stated facts to follow along with; the -->
<!-- arguments live in the instructor notes below. The second half (chain rule exploration) is -->
<!-- genuine group work. -->

<!-- Deliberately AVOIDS: the exponential function e^x and the inverse relationship between exp -->
<!-- and ln, general (non-integer) real exponents, and the general power rule for derivatives. The -->
<!-- log power rule is motivated ONLY for positive integer exponents via repeated use of the -->
<!-- product rule; irrational and general real exponents wait until exp is defined (then -->
<!-- a^p := e^(p ln a) makes ln(a^p) = p ln(a) immediate). Also avoids any proof that the -->
<!-- scaling observation holds for b < 1 (the same term-by-term matching works, with orientation -->
<!-- reversed); state a, b > 0 and demonstrate with b > 1. -->

<!-- Lecture notes, for instructor reference (not shown to students): -->
<!--   ln(1): int_1^1 (1/t) dt = 0, zero-width interval. -->
<!--   Domain: 1/t is not defined at t=0 and the integral from 1 cannot cross that -->
<!--     discontinuity, so x must stay in (0, infinity). -->
<!--   Scaling observation: int_a^(ab) (1/t) dt = int_1^b (1/t) dt for a, b > 0. -->
<!--     Right Riemann sum for the left side with n subintervals: Delta t = (ab-a)/n = a(b-1)/n, -->
<!--     t_k = a + k*a(b-1)/n = a(1 + k(b-1)/n). Then (1/t_k)*Delta t = -->
<!--     [1/(a(1+k(b-1)/n))] * a(b-1)/n = (1/(1+k(b-1)/n)) * (b-1)/n. -->
<!--     The right side has Delta x = (b-1)/n, x_k = 1 + k(b-1)/n, so (1/x_k)*Delta x is the SAME -->
<!--     expression. The a cancels term by term, so the two Riemann sums are identical for every -->
<!--     n, and hence so are the limits. Geometric picture: the rectangles on [a,ab] are the -->
<!--     rectangles on [1,b] stretched horizontally by a and squashed vertically by 1/a, so the -->
<!--     areas are unchanged. -->
<!--   Product: int_1^(ab) = int_1^a + int_a^(ab) = ln(a) + int_1^b (scaling) = ln(a) + ln(b). -->
<!--   Reciprocal: 1 = b*(1/b), so 0 = ln(1) = ln(b) + ln(1/b), hence ln(1/b) = -ln(b). -->
<!--   Quotient: ln(a/b) = ln(a * (1/b)) = ln(a) + ln(1/b) = ln(a) - ln(b). -->
<!--   Integer powers: ln(a^2) = ln(a*a) = 2 ln(a); ln(a^3) = ln(a*a^2) = ln(a) + 2 ln(a) = -->
<!--     3 ln(a); in general ln(a^p) = p ln(a) for positive integers p. -->

<!-- Answer key, for instructor reference (not shown to students): -->
<!--   task-chain-3x: d/dx ln(3x) = (1/(3x))*3 = 1/x. Domain: 3x > 0, so x > 0. -->
<!--   task-chain-neg4x: d/dx ln(-4x) = (1/(-4x))*(-4) = 1/x. Domain: -4x > 0, so x < 0. -->
<!--   task-chain-negx: d/dx ln(-x) = (1/(-x))*(-1) = 1/x. Domain: -x > 0, so x < 0. -->
<!--   task-chain-power: for a positive integer p, d/dx ln(x^p) = (1/x^p)*p x^(p-1) = p/x. -->
<!--     Domain: x^p > 0. For odd p this is x > 0. For even p this is all x != 0. This agrees with -->
<!--     the derivative of p*ln(x), as the integer power rule predicts. -->
<!--   task-chain-general: d/dx ln(g(x)) = g'(x)/g(x), wherever g(x) > 0. -->
<!--   task-chain-practice: d/dx ln(x^2+1) = 2x/(x^2+1). Domain is all real numbers, since -->
<!--     x^2+1 > 0 always. -->
<!--   task-chain-pattern: every example except the power case gives 1/x, including on the negative -->
<!--     side of the axis. The negative-side results (ln(-x), ln(-4x)) show the derivative of ln of -->
<!--     a NEGATIVE-argument expression is still 1/x, which is what lets ln|x| work for x < 0. -->
<!--   Punchline: for x > 0, |x| = x so d/dx ln|x| = d/dx ln(x) = 1/x. For x < 0, |x| = -x so -->
<!--     d/dx ln|x| = d/dx ln(-x) = 1/x (the ln(-x) task). So d/dx ln|x| = 1/x for all x != 0, and -->
<!--     int (1/x) dx = ln|x| + C on any interval avoiding 0. -->

<!-- Suggested pacing for a ~50 minute class: -->
<!--   Intro + definition + ln(1) and domain remark ............................ ~5  min -->
<!--   Lecture: scaling observation (Riemann sums, geometric picture) .......... ~8  min -->
<!--   Lecture: product, reciprocal, quotient rules ............................ ~7  min -->
<!--   Lecture: integer powers ................................................. ~3  min -->
<!--   Exercise 1: chain rule with ln (3x, -4x, -x, x^p) ....................... ~12 min -->
<!--   Exercise 1 wrap-up: general rule + practice task ........................ ~6  min -->
<!--   Punchline: ln|x|, cases for |x|, and the antiderivative of 1/x ........... ~6  min -->
<!--   TOTAL ..................................................................... ~47 min -->

<!-- Where to cut if you're running short (in order of preference): -->
<!--   1. Drop task-chain-practice (x^2+1); the general rule already covers it. -->
<!--   2. Skip the integer-power lecture demonstration beyond ln(a^2) and ln(a^3), and just state -->
<!--      the pattern. -->
<!--   3. In Exercise 1, assign ln(-x) as follow-up ONLY IF ln(-4x) is done; keep at least one -->
<!--      negative-argument example, because the punchline depends on it. -->
<!--   4. State the quotient rule without dwelling on it; it is a one-line corollary. -->
<!--   5. Do NOT cut the scaling observation, the product rule, the negative-argument chain rule -->
<!--      example, or the ln|x| punchline: these are the core of the day. -->

<worksheet xml:id="activity-logarithm-as-integral">
  <title>Properties of the Logarithm Defined by an Integral</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-logarithm-as-integral">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Explain why <m>\ln(1)=0</m> and why the domain of <m>\displaystyle\ln(x)=\int_1^x\frac{1}{t}\,dt</m> is <m>(0,\infty)</m>, directly from the definition.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize that <m>\displaystyle\int_a^{ab}\frac{1}{t}\,dt=\int_1^b\frac{1}{t}\,dt</m> by comparing Riemann sums. Use this to justify <m>\ln(ab)=\ln(a)+\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the product rule for <m>\ln</m> to obtain the reciprocal rule, the quotient rule, and the power rule <m>\ln(a^p)=p\ln(a)</m> for positive integers <m>p</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Apply the chain rule to differentiate <m>\ln</m> of a linear or polynomial expression, and determine the domain of the result.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain why <m>\displaystyle\frac{d}{dx}\big[\ln|x|\big]=\frac{1}{x}</m> for all <m>x\ne 0</m>.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Last time we used the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to create a new function that
      no power rule could produce. Today we find out what it can do. Everything we learn about
      this function will come from its definition as an integral, not from any formula or from
      anything you may remember about logarithms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Team Roles: Manager, Recorder, Spokesperson, and Reflector.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <definition xml:id="def-natural-log-integral">
    <title>The Natural Logarithm</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>x\gt 0</m>, the <term>natural logarithm</term> is defined by
        <md>\ln(x) = \int_1^x \frac{1}{t}\,dt\text{.}</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ln-two-facts">
    <title>Two Facts Straight from the Definition</title>
    <p>
      First, <m>\ln(1)=\displaystyle\int_1^1\frac{1}{t}\,dt=0</m>, since an integral over an
      interval of zero width is zero. Second, the integrand <m>1/t</m> is not defined at
      <m>t=0</m>, and an integral starting at <m>t=1</m> cannot pass through that discontinuity.
      So the domain of <m>\ln(x)</m> is <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ln-scaling-observation">
    <title>Key Observation: Scaling the Interval</title>
    <p>
      For any <m>a\gt 0</m> and <m>b\gt 0</m>,
      <md>\int_a^{ab}\frac{1}{t}\,dt = \int_1^{b}\frac{1}{t}\,dt\text{.}</md>
      The reason is that the right Riemann sum for the left integral and the right Riemann sum
      for the right integral, with the same number of subintervals <m>n</m>, are exactly the
      same expression. The factor <m>a</m> in the width of each subinterval cancels the factor
      <m>a</m> in each endpoint, term by term, so the two sums agree for every <m>n</m>, and
      therefore so do their limits.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-ln-algebraic-properties">
    <title>Algebraic Properties of <m>\ln</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For all <m>a\gt 0</m> and <m>b\gt 0</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><em>Product rule:</em> <m>\ln(ab)=\ln(a)+\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Reciprocal rule:</em> <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{1}{b}\right)=-\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Quotient rule:</em> <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{a}{b}\right)=\ln(a)-\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ln-integer-powers">
    <title>Powers</title>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>p</m> is a positive integer, so that <m>a^p</m> means
      <m>a\cdot a\cdots a</m> with <m>p</m> factors. Applying the product rule once for each
      factor gives
      <md>\ln(a^p)=\ln(a)+\ln(a)+\cdots+\ln(a)=p\,\ln(a)\text{.}</md>
      For now we only claim this for integers <m>p</m>. Other exponents require additional
      arguments.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-chain-rule-with-ln" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Chain Rule with <m>\ln</m></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus tells us that
        <m>\displaystyle\frac{d}{dx}\big[\ln(x)\big]=\frac{1}{x}</m> for <m>x\gt 0</m>. Use this
        together with the chain rule to differentiate each function below. For each one, also
        determine the domain, meaning the values of <m>x</m> for which the expression inside
        the <m>\ln</m> is positive.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-chain-3x" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the derivative of <m>\ln(3x)</m> and state its domain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-chain-neg4x" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the derivative of <m>\ln(-4x)</m> and state its domain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-chain-negx" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the derivative of <m>\ln(-x)</m> and state its domain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-chain-pattern" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Look back at your answers forthe derivatives of <m>\ln(3x)</m>, <m>\ln(-4x)</m>, and <m>\ln(-x)</m>.
          What do you notice? How are they different?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-absolute-value-recall">
    <title>Recall: Absolute Value</title>
    <p>
      The absolute value of <m>x</m> is the piecewise function
      <md>|x| = \begin{cases} x \amp \text{if } x\gt 0 \\ -x \amp \text{if } x\lt 0 \end{cases}</md>
      so <m>|x|</m> is positive for every <m>x\ne 0</m>. This means <m>\ln|x|</m> makes sense for
      every <m>x\ne 0</m>, not just for <m>x\gt 0</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-derivative-ln-absolute-value">
    <title>The Derivative of <m>\ln|x|</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For all <m>x\ne 0</m>,
        <md>\frac{d}{dx}\big[\ln|x|\big]=\frac{1}{x}\text{.}</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-generalize-ln-chain-rule" workspace="0in">
    
    <task xml:id="task-chain-power" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p</m> be an integer. Find the derivative of <m>\ln(x^p)</m>. 
          How does the domain depend on whether <m>p</m> is even or odd?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-chain-power-revisit" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Repeat the derivative of <m>\ln(x^p)</m> but first use the power rule for logarithms.
          How does this compare to the previous problem?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-chain-general" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>g(x)</m> is a differentiable function. Write a general rule for
          <m>\displaystyle\frac{d}{dx}\big[\ln(|g(x)|)\big]</m>, and say where it applies.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-chain-practice1" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your rule to differentiate <m>\ln(x^2+1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-chain-practice2" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your rule to differentiate <m>\ln(|x^3-2x^2|)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
